---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RegexTester from '../../components/RegexTester.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Regex Tester" description="Test a regular expression against sample text and see live matches.">
  <RegexTester client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why testing beats guessing</h2>
  <p>A regex pattern that looks correct can still fail on real input in ways that are hard to predict just by reading it — testing against actual sample text catches false positives and false negatives before the pattern ships in application code.</p>

  <h2>How to use this tester</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your regex pattern and any flags (like <code>g</code> or <code>i</code>).</li>
    <li>Paste sample text to test against.</li>
    <li>See matches highlighted live as you edit the pattern.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Regex Testing Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Testing against only one example input</strong>, missing edge cases like empty strings, extra whitespace, or unexpected casing that real-world data often contains.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting the global flag</strong> when you need every match in the text, not just the first one.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Regex Tester FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this validate regex syntax across all languages?</strong> It uses JavaScript's regex engine — syntax is very similar across most languages, but some features (like certain lookbehind support) can differ, so verify in your actual runtime for anything unusual.</p>
  <p><strong>Is my text sent anywhere?</strong> No — matching runs entirely in your browser using JavaScript's built-in regex engine.</p>
</ToolLayout>
